1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,760
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 2, Minds and Other Bodies. In 2002, researchers at Oxford published a short paper in Science about a new Caledonian crow named Betty. Betty and a male companion, Abel, were offered a little bucket of food at the bottom of a vertical tube, along with two pieces of wire, one straight and one hooked. Abel flew off with the hook. Betty picked up the straight wire, wedged one end, bent it into a hook, and fished out the bucket. She did it again on later trials.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,760
It looked like insight, a bird solving a new problem by making a tool it had never been taught to make.

3
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:40,560
Fourteen years later, a team led by Christian Rutz complicated the story.

4
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:47,920
Some wild-caught New Caledonian crows, kept briefly in field aviaries, bent straight twigs without needing to,

5
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,600
and one of them used Betty's exact wedge-and-pull technique.

6
00:00:51,880 --> 00:00:58,740
Bending may simply be part of the species' toolkit, so Betty may have been running a routine rather than having a flash of insight.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,540
did not rule inside out. Either way, the story carries the lesson of this whole episode.

8
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,380
Here's the argument. Intelligence has evolved again and again in very different bodies and

9
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:17,060
brains, in birds, in octopuses, in insects, and in simpler forms in cells and collectives.

10
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:22,520
It is a family of solutions to the problems of staying alive, not a ladder with us at the top.

11
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:28,400
And when we study it, we err in both directions. We see minds that are not there, and we miss

12
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,700
Start with the two errors.

13
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:37,440
In the early 1900s, a horse in Berlin called Clever Hands appeared to do arithmetic,

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:39,600
tapping out answers with a hoof.

15
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:45,880
The psychologist Oskar Funxt showed that Hands was reading tiny, involuntary cues in his

16
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:49,460
questioner's posture, which relaxed when he reached the right number.

17
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:52,340
Hands was clever, just not at arithmetic.

18
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:58,160
The corrective principle, called Morgan's Canon after the psychologist Conway Lloyd Morgan,

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
to a higher faculty if a lower one would explain it.

20
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:07,480
Clever hands is the founding parable of machine learning evaluation.

21
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:13,000
Two, a model that scores well by exploiting a spurious cue in its data

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,840
is literally called a clever hands predictor.

23
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:18,180
And he'll be back when we get to machines.

24
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:21,340
The opposite error is refusing to credit a mind

25
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,300
even when the evidence is strong.

26
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:27,560
The primatologist Franz Duval called it anthropodennial

27
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,940
and argued that the history of animal cognition is largely a history of humans testing animals badly

28
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,940
and then underestimating them.

29
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,440
Part of the problem is what the biologist Jakob von Uck School called the umwelt,

30
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,800
an animal's own perceptual world.

31
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:49,900
A tick waiting on a branch responds to little more than the smell of mammal skin, warmth, and the texture of hair.

32
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:56,760
A test designed around human senses and human interests can easily miss what another animal is good at.

33
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:01,060
Every result in this episode comes with the same question attached.

34
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,860
Which of the two errors is it most at risk of?

35
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:06,320
Begin with the birds.

36
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:09,540
New Caledonian crows do not just use tools.

37
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:14,220
In 2018, a team showed them assembling compound tools,

38
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:18,040
fitting short pieces together into one long enough to reach a reward,

39
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:19,740
without having been shown how.

40
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:21,380
Ravens appeared a plan.

41
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:28,040
In 2017, Kankabadaya and Mathias Asfath showed that ravens would select a tool

42
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:30,700
or a token they could later trade for food

43
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:34,020
and keep it for use after a delay as long as a night,

44
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:37,640
performing about as well as great apes on similar tasks.

45
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:40,480
And scrub jays seemed to model other minds.

46
00:03:40,720 --> 00:03:44,340
In a classic study by Nathan Emery and Nicola Clayton,

47
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:49,120
jays that had been watched while hiding food often came back alone and moved it,

48
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:55,360
but only if they had stolen from others themselves. Experienced thieves seem to anticipate theft.

49
00:03:55,660 --> 00:04:01,800
How do birds manage this with brains the size of a walnut? Part of the answer came in 2016,

50
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:09,000
when Sueren Olkovich, Susanna Herculano, Uzel, and colleagues counted neurons in the brains of

51
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,480
28 bird species. Parrots and songbirds pack neurons about twice as densely as primates with

52
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:22,000
brains of the same mass. A large macaws forebrain holds at least as many neurons as a macaque monkey's

53
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,860
cortex, in a brain a fraction of the size. Corvids still fall well short of great apes,

54
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:34,040
so the claim is not that crows equal chimpanzees. It's that a small brain can carry a great deal of

55
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:39,520
computing power. Birds also lack the layered cortex that mammals have, which long made their

56
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:46,460
intelligence look like a puzzle. In 2020, Martin Stasho, Honor Gunterkun, and colleagues found that

57
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:51,620
parts of the bird forebrain contain repeating, column-like circuits strikingly similar to the

58
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:58,900
organization of our cortex, though not in every region. Then, in February 2025, a set of papers

59
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:04,180
in Science traced how these bird circuits develop. The circuits arise from different developmental

60
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:10,180
origins and mostly different gene programs than the mammalian cortex, though a few neuron types

61
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:16,660
do look shared. Birds and mammals last shared an ancestor around 320 million years ago.

62
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:22,040
What they share is not an inherited cortex. It's largely a convergent solution,

63
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:27,760
arrived at twice. Biologists treat convergence as nature's own replication study.

64
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:31,260
Eyes evolved many times, and so did flight.

65
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:34,660
When unrelated lineages arrive at similar answers,

66
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:39,040
it suggests the answer is shaped by the problem more than by the ancestry.

67
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:43,680
That is a claim about organization, not about any particular tissue.

68
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:47,040
The octopus pushes the point further.

69
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:51,880
Octopuses are mollusks, closer to clams and snails than to us,

70
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,640
and the last common ancestor of octopuses and humans lived perhaps 600 million years ago,

71
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:01,040
Probably a small, simple, worm-like animal.

72
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:06,260
Everything sophisticated about octopus cognition evolved separately from ours.

73
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:08,900
Separately is doing a lot of work there.

74
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:14,980
Octopuses still have neurons, synapses, and many of the same molecular parts we do.

75
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:17,360
True, and it's worth being precise.

76
00:06:17,700 --> 00:06:21,620
The building blocks are shared, inherited from that distant ancestor.

77
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:26,200
Nerve cells, the basic signaling machinery, many of the same genes.

78
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:34,140
was sequenced in 2015, it even showed a large expansion of a gene family called the protocaterines,

79
00:06:34,380 --> 00:06:40,020
which help wire up neurons, a family that had also expanded, independently, in vertebrates.

80
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:45,620
What evolved separately is the large-scale architecture. How many neurons, how they're

81
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:52,140
organized, and what they do. Same bricks, different buildings. An octopus has about 500 million

82
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:56,760
neurons, and about two-thirds of them are in its arms rather than its central brain.

83
00:06:56,940 --> 00:07:03,240
The arms carry out complex actions semi-independently. Octopuses solve puzzles,

84
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:08,740
escape from tanks, recognize individual people, and appear to play. Their relatives,

85
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:15,600
cuttlefish, passed a version of the marshmallow test in a 2021 study led by Alexandra Schnell.

86
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:19,780
They would pass up a less preferred food to get a preferred one later,

87
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:23,000
waiting anywhere from under a minute to more than two minutes,

88
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:26,180
and the best waiters were also the fastest learners.

89
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:28,760
There's a puzzle in their life history, too.

90
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:33,940
Most octopuses live only a year or two, and many die soon after breeding,

91
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:36,180
so the young never meet their parents.

92
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:41,120
In our own lineage, big brains go with long lives and long childhoods.

93
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:44,140
A 2019 review by Piro Amodio,

94
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:48,300
Nicola Clayton and colleagues suggested that losing the protective shell

95
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:55,680
Once exposed, these animals needed flexible ways to hunt and to avoid being eaten.

96
00:07:56,200 --> 00:08:02,060
Whatever the right answer turns out to be, it's a different route to a large brain under different pressures,

97
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:07,720
which is exactly what you'd expect if intelligence is a family of solutions rather than a ladder.

98
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:12,540
The philosopher Peter Godfrey Smith, who has spent years diving with octopuses,

99
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:17,840
wrote that meeting one is probably the closest we will come to meeting an intelligent alien.

100
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:25,960
octopus, in the philosophy of episode 9, the evidence of episode 10, and the machine debates

101
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:31,600
of episodes 12 and 13, because it shows that complex cognition can be built on a radically

102
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:38,640
different plan. Insects push the size question to an extreme. A honeybee brain has about a million

103
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:45,060
neurons, nearly a hundred thousand times fewer than ours. Yet honeybees perform the waggle dance,

104
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:50,560
the symbolic communication of direction and distance that won Carl von Frisch a share of

105
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:58,540
the 1973 Nobel Prize. How can so few neurons do so much? In a 2009 review, Lars Chitka and

106
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:04,080
Jeremy Niven argued that many abilities we associate with big brains need surprisingly

107
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:10,300
small circuits, and that bigger brains often buy more detail, more memory, and more parallel

108
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:15,920
processing, rather than qualitatively new kinds of thinking. Bumblebees show something closer to

109
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:23,340
culture. In 2016, Sylvain Allem, Lars Chitka, and colleagues hid an artificial flower under a clear

110
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:31,160
plastic sheet, reachable only by pulling a string. Left to themselves, just two of 291 bees solved it.

111
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:36,020
But most bees that watched a trained demonstrator learned it, and the skills spread through

112
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:42,140
colonies. The mechanism matters, though. Observers seem to have been drawn to the string, and then

113
00:09:42,140 --> 00:09:47,920
to have learned by trial and error. That is not imitation of the pulling movement, and Morgan's

114
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:54,920
canon says to take that reading seriously. In 2024, Chitka's group went further. They built a

115
00:09:54,920 --> 00:10:01,520
two-step puzzle box that no bee solved on its own, over 12 days or more of access, yet several bees

116
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:06,280
learned it from trained demonstrators, that social learning of a behavior too complex

117
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:11,200
to invent alone, which in humans we treat as the seed of cumulative culture.

118
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:19,240
And in 2022, the same lab reported that bumblebees rolled small balls over and over with no reward

119
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:23,100
and came to prefer a colored chamber they associated with the balls.

120
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:24,860
Play in an insect.

121
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:27,780
That's exactly where I'd reach for Morgan's cannon.

122
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:30,180
So did the authors, by design.

123
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:37,580
standard criteria animal behaviorists use for play. The behavior is voluntary, not directly

124
00:10:37,580 --> 00:10:43,220
rewarded, repeated, and done when the animal is relaxed rather than stressed. Whether anything

125
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:51,060
is enjoyed is a question for part two. As behavior, it met the criteria. Is there a general intelligence

126
00:10:51,060 --> 00:10:58,000
in animals, like human G? Within species, increasingly yes. Louis Matzel and colleagues

127
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,760
found that mice good at one kind of learning tend to be good at others, with a general factor

128
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:07,380
accounting for roughly two-fifths of the differences on their test batteries. Across

129
00:11:07,380 --> 00:11:12,920
species, Simon Reeder, Ifka Hodger, and Kevin Laland compiled reports of innovation,

130
00:11:13,380 --> 00:11:18,940
social learning, and tool use for 62 primate species, and found that a single dimension

131
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:25,740
captured most of the variation, with high scores evolving independently in several lineages—capuchins,

132
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:32,720
baboons, macaques, and great apes. In a 2017 paper in the journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences,

133
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:38,520
Judith Burkhart, Michael Schubiger, and Karel van Skijk drew these strands together,

134
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:44,720
arguing that general intelligence is not unique to humans and linking its evolution to how much

135
00:11:44,720 --> 00:11:49,880
animals learn from one another. But the measurement problems are serious, and they're the problems of

136
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:55,260
episode one in new clothes. In animal studies, being keen on food can look like being clever.

137
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:59,700
Samples are often small and drawn from a single laboratory strain,

138
00:11:59,880 --> 00:12:02,000
and species differ in what they're good at.

139
00:12:02,260 --> 00:12:07,100
In 2007, Esther Hermann and colleagues gave the same broad battery

140
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:11,140
to two-and-a-half-year-old children, chimpanzees, and orangutans.

141
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:14,840
On physical problems about tools, quantities, and space,

142
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:17,500
the toddlers and the apes performed similarly.

143
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:22,720
On social problems, such as following someone's gaze or learning by watching,

144
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:24,700
the toddlers were far ahead.

145
00:12:25,740 --> 00:12:30,880
do not sit at the top of a single ladder. We are unusually specialized for learning from each other.

146
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:38,700
Brains tell the same mixed story. In 2014, Evan McLean and dozens of collaborators tested self-control

147
00:12:38,700 --> 00:12:46,420
in 567 animals from 36 species. Absolute brain size predicted performance better than brain size

148
00:12:46,420 --> 00:12:52,060
relative to the body. And among primates, the breadth of a species diet predicted self-control,

149
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:54,540
while the size of its social groups did not.

150
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:57,280
Different pressures produce different profiles.

151
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:00,620
Now go below the level of brains.

152
00:13:00,980 --> 00:13:04,820
The biologist Michael Levin studies what he calls basal cognition,

153
00:13:05,180 --> 00:13:07,540
problem-solving by cells and tissues.

154
00:13:08,060 --> 00:13:12,880
His framework, called TAME, borrows an old criterion from William James

155
00:13:12,880 --> 00:13:17,220
that intelligence is the ability to reach the same goal by different means.

156
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:20,060
Levin's lab studies bioelectricity,

157
00:13:22,060 --> 00:13:28,280
cell membranes that help coordinate how tissues grow. Flatworms called planaria can regrow a whole

158
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:34,240
body from a fragment. In one experiment, briefly blocking the electrical connections between cells

159
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:39,580
made some fragments regrow with two heads, and those worms kept regrowing two heads when cut

160
00:13:39,580 --> 00:13:44,660
again, with no further treatment. Even some treated worms that looked normal grew two heads

161
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:50,340
when they were cut later, as if a stored pattern had been rewritten. Levin's group has also built

162
00:13:52,060 --> 00:13:56,920
and anthrobots from human airway cells, small living constructs that move on their own.

163
00:13:57,160 --> 00:14:01,980
What they show about cognition, as opposed to the remarkable plasticity of development,

164
00:14:02,260 --> 00:14:08,180
is still argued over. Even a single cell can solve spatial problems. The slime mold

165
00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:14,080
Fisarum polycephalum, one giant cell with many nuclei, finds short paths through mazes.

166
00:14:14,360 --> 00:14:20,680
In a 2010 experiment, researchers placed oat flakes in the positions of the cities around Tokyo,

167
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:32,500
That's a distributed optimizer. I'm not sure I'd call it intelligent.

168
00:14:32,980 --> 00:14:36,220
That's the live debate, and it's partly about vocabulary.

169
00:14:37,020 --> 00:14:43,020
Levin would say intelligence comes in degrees, how well a system navigates toward goals in some space,

170
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:46,080
whether physical, anatomical, or behavioral.

171
00:14:46,680 --> 00:14:50,460
Critics reply that stretching the word to slime molds empties it.

172
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:52,740
about the phenomena.

173
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:56,400
Plants are where that disagreement gets heated

174
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:59,300
and where a failed replication is instructive.

175
00:14:59,640 --> 00:15:03,820
A century ago, the Indian physicist Jagadish Chandra Bose

176
00:15:03,820 --> 00:15:05,480
built sensitive instruments

177
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:08,000
to record electrical responses in plants

178
00:15:08,280 --> 00:15:11,160
and argued that they had something like nervous reactions,

179
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:13,880
ideas that were partly ahead of their time

180
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:15,400
and partly overstated.

181
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:18,880
Today, we know that plants signal electrically and chemically.

182
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:25,300
A venous flytrap effectively counts touches, snapping shut after two and starting digestion

183
00:15:25,300 --> 00:15:31,160
after more. The sensitive plant mimosa stops folding its leaves when it is repeatedly dropped

184
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:37,980
without harm, which looks like habituation. In 2016, Monica Gagliano and colleagues reported

185
00:15:37,980 --> 00:15:42,380
that pea seedlings could learn to associate a breeze with light, a form of conditioning.

186
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:49,580
In 2020, a replication by the plant biologist Casey Markle found no evidence of it.

187
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:53,520
The original team argued that his setup differed in key ways,

188
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:57,140
and he replied that those differences could not explain the result.

189
00:15:57,340 --> 00:16:04,020
And in 2019, Lincoln Tays and colleagues argued that plants neither possess nor require consciousness.

190
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:09,300
Most plant scientists accept sophisticated signaling and adaptive behavior.

191
00:16:09,600 --> 00:16:12,260
Far fewer accept talk of plant minds.

192
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:18,460
Here the error at risk was overtribution, and so far the claim has not held up under independent testing.

193
00:16:19,660 --> 00:16:21,720
Finally, mines made of many.

194
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:28,800
A honeybee swarm chooses a new home through a process the biologist Thomas Seeley described in Honeybee Democracy.

195
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:36,280
Scouts advertise candidate sites by dancing, support builds, and the swarm commits when a quorum forms,

196
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:41,100
a process that resembles the way neurons accumulate evidence toward a decision.

197
00:16:42,380 --> 00:16:47,700
may have something similar. Anita Woolley and colleagues have proposed a collective intelligence

198
00:16:47,700 --> 00:16:54,540
factor for groups, and a 2021 meta-analysis found that how a group collaborates predicts that factor

199
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:59,760
about twice as well as the skills of its members, though whether groups really have a general factor

200
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:08,280
at all is still argued over. Crows, octopuses, bees, slime molds, swarms. What ties the menagerie

201
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:15,160
together. Organization. Intelligence is multiply realized. It evolved separately in birds,

202
00:17:15,460 --> 00:17:21,300
cephalopods, and insects, on different body plans and brain architectures, and evolution keeps

203
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:26,680
converging on similar solutions. That's one of the strongest arguments that what matters is

204
00:17:26,680 --> 00:17:32,280
organization rather than any one kind of tissue, and we'll test that argument hard in parts two

205
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:38,620
and three. It's a family, not a ladder. Species differ in profile, and the human specialty seems

206
00:17:38,620 --> 00:17:44,400
to be learning from one another. And our methods err both ways. Clever hands, the doubts about

207
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:50,400
Betty, and the pea seedlings warn against seeing too much. Anthropodennial, the neuron counts and

208
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:55,520
the bees warn against seeing too little. The same tension will haunt every claim about machines.

209
00:17:55,900 --> 00:18:01,160
So if intelligence takes this many forms, can it be defined at all, without reference to any

210
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:06,900
body, or test. That's the next episode, Intelligence Formally.

